@amber_wren37: Biggest win for us was profiling before touching anything. cProfile or py-spy will usually show one step eating 80% of the time, and it's rarely the one you guessed.

Then the usual suspects: swap row-wise apply() for vectorised pandas or numpy ops, stream data in chunks instead of loading it all, and use pyarrow or parquet instead of CSV where you can.

If the pipeline touches hardware, like sensors or lab rigs, run timing tests against the real device too. Simulated throughput can hide I/O stalls that only show up on the bench.

